[G9] excision-width asymmetry: is the PRINCIPAL-VALUE IMPLEMENTATION numerically stable?
     Measured: widen the late side of the excision by a factor `asym`, at several excision widths.
     Scanned in delta (this tests the PV implementation, NOT the extension parameter -- see G10):
     LQC      dR/dln(asym) at delta/eta_B = 3e-3, 1e-3, 3e-4, 1e-4:  -8.8080e-04, -2.9360e-04, -8.8074e-05, -2.9368e-05
              slope/delta = 0.294, 0.294, 0.294, 0.294  -> constant to 3.6e-04, i.e. EXACTLY linear in delta
     poly     dR/dln(asym) at delta/eta_B = 3e-3, 1e-3, 3e-4, 1e-4:  -7.2653e-04, -2.4217e-04, -7.2616e-05, -2.4216e-05
              slope/delta = 0.242, 0.242, 0.242, 0.242  -> constant to 5.0e-04, i.e. EXACTLY linear in delta
     -> PASS: proportional to the excision width, so the PRINCIPAL-VALUE IMPLEMENTATION is stable.
        RETRACTION: an earlier version of this lane read that as 'the continuation is unique'.
        It is not. Widening one side of the excision moves within the principal-value family only;
        it does NOT probe the self-adjoint-extension parameter of the singular operator, which is
        the actual freedom. That is measured in G10, and it is O(1). G9 now claims only what it
        tests: the PV implementation is numerically stable.

[G10] the SELF-ADJOINT-EXTENSION freedom. At a simple sign-changing zero of z^2 = 2a^2 eps the
      Mukhanov-Sasaki operator has z''/z = -1/(4u^2), the critical inverse-square potential: it is
      NOT essentially self-adjoint, and admits a one-parameter family of extensions per surface.
      (a) complex contour 1/Q -> 1/(Q - i mu), mu -> 0 -- the analytic-continuation extension:
      LQC      R_PV = 0.499958 | 1/(Q - i mu): 0.692227, 0.702620, 0.705610, 0.706658, 0.706958 -> 0.706958  (adjudication predicted 0.707048)
               real extension nu = -2,-1,0,1,2: 0.18165, 0.34080, 0.49996, 0.65911, 0.81827
               R(nu) is LINEAR in nu, and R = 1 -- i.e. INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM SCHEME S1 --
               at nu = +3.1419  (= pi to 4 digits)
      poly     R_PV = 0.374966 | 1/(Q - i mu): 0.713544, 0.738956, 0.746312, 0.748894, 0.749633 -> 0.749633  (adjudication predicted 0.749932)
               real extension nu = -2,-1,0,1,2: 0.03853, 0.16822, 0.37497, 0.58171, 0.78846
               R(nu) is LINEAR in nu, and R = 1 -- i.e. INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM SCHEME S1 --
               at nu = +3.0232
      (b) LQC ONLY -- an anchor that needs no prescription at all. The MATTER-KINETIC weight
          z_K^2 = a^2(rho+p)/(c_s^2 H^2) = 3a^2/(1-x) is STRICTLY POSITIVE: it has no zero, because
          on this background the zero of 2a^2 eps sits at 1-2x = 0, where the quantum-geometry
          factor vanishes, NOT where the matter kinetic term does (rho+p = x never vanishes).
          I_K = int deta/z_K^2 = 0.3022998940 = pi/(6 sqrt3) -- IDENTICAL to the principal value of the
          geometric mixing integral -- so R = 3 I_K/I_S1 = 0.500000 = 1/2 with NO prescription.
          poly has no matter sector specified, so no such anchor exists there and 3/8 is PV-only.

[H] the two handoff conventions for converting R into a transfer coefficient:
    A = common DUST handoff, where zeta^Bardeen == zeta^S1 (gate G8) -> T = T_S1/R exactly;
    B = the paper's handoff at -eta_B, each scheme using its own zeta there (Bardeen: log finite part).
    On the Quintin-type background -eta_B IS the end of the exact dust phase, so A and B must coincide;
    on LQC/poly -eta_B is the Q = 0 surface, where eps = Q/Hc^2 = 0 EXACTLY -- so the matter-contraction
    value f_NL^before = -35/16 (an eps = 3/2 result) cannot be evaluated there at all, and zeta is
    additionally log-divergent. Convention A is therefore the defensible one; B is reported anyway.
    quintin  T_S1 0.1650 | A 1.0310 | B 1.0310 | B/A 1.000 | log/finite-part 0.00 | eps at handoff 1.500
    LQC      T_S1 0.2500 | A 0.5000 | B 0.3465 | B/A 0.693 | log/finite-part 0.46 | eps at handoff 0.000
    poly     T_S1 0.1955 | A 0.5213 | B 0.0975 | B/A 0.187 | log/finite-part 2.83 | eps at handoff 0.000
    -> the two conventions agree to 4.4e-06 on the Quintin-type background, where the handoff is
       legitimate; they differ by the log contamination elsewhere. Convention A is adopted.

